#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""wiki-crosslink — find the edges a source-backed page owes but has not written.

Pure stdlib, no dependencies, per repo convention.

Subcommands:
  entities [filter]   The entity index: every wiki page's page-name, aliases and
                      infobox handles, i.e. every string that can be looked for
                      in a source.
  coverage [prefix]   Which readable corpora actually name a page, and how
                      often. The triage step — run it before scanning, because
                      most page/corpus pairs are silent and finding that out
                      costs ~3s for the whole wiki against all three corpora.
                      `--silent` inverts it: the pages nothing names, with the
                      ones that cite a corpus anyway called out.
  scan <page>         Unlinked entities in the corpora a page's `sources:` cite.
                      `--against <corpus>` scans a corpus the page does NOT
                      cite, filtered to rows that name the page itself.
                      `--queue [n] --against <c>` is the campaign queue, pages
                      that state a limit about their own evidence first.
                      `--low` includes single-token name matches (mostly noise).
                      `--all` over the twitter year pages.
  reciprocal [page]   Outbound typed edges whose target does not link back. The
                      inverse-edge obligation of CONNECTIONS_SPEC.md, computed
                      rather than remembered.
  orphaned            Pages whose `sources:` name a corpus this tool can read
                      and which link nothing mined from it — the pages where a
                      scan has never been run.
  check               Gate: reports the reciprocal debt as a warning banner and
                      exits 0. Never blocks a commit; see below.

Why this exists
---------------
A page assembled from a corpus records what its writer found *interesting*.
That is not the same set as what the corpus *contains*, and the gap between
them is invisible from the page — which is exactly the condition CLAUDE.md's
"depth is the binding constraint" describes. Nineteen twitter year pages were
written by reading every tweet in each year, and they carry 130 typed edges
between them; the archive names people, places, works and concepts that have
their own wiki pages several times that often. Every one of those is a
connection nobody can make later, because synthesis reasons from `wiki/` and
the mention never reached it.

This tool does not write edges and must not be made to. It produces
CANDIDATES, and a candidate is a reason to go and read the source rows it
names. `skills/corpus/vocabulary-drift.md` is the standing warning about what a
string match over a long corpus measures: a count is a measurement of the
matcher's vocabulary, and it is worthless as a finding. It is not worthless as
a pointer, which is the only thing used here — every row this prints carries the
dated source lines that produced it, so the verification step is reading them,
never trusting the count.

Three properties of the matching, stated because they decide how a result is
read:

1.  **Confidence is a property of the string, not of the match.** An @handle or
    a two-token full name is unambiguous; a bare first name ("Bill", "Al",
    "Jess") matches any Bill. Single-token names are reported at `low` and are
    never to be acted on without reading the row.
2.  **A mention is not a relationship.** The archive naming a person is
    evidence the page may owe an edge, and nothing more. A tweet that mentions
    Diplo is not a Diplo relationship; it is a music-consumption datapoint that
    may belong on a different page entirely. The type and the claim come from
    reading, per CONNECTIONS_SPEC.md.
3.  **Absence here is not absence in the corpus.** The index can only look for
    names somebody already wrote a page for. Entities with no page are exactly
    the ones a scan cannot see, and finding them is a reading job.
4.  **A string two pages both claim is evidence for neither.** `@alexisarmel`
    is in one person's `aliases:` and in another's infobox `handles:`, so 85
    rows about the first were offered as 101 mentions of the second. Every such
    candidate is now marked `contested` with the competing name, because the
    index knows and the output used to not say.
5.  **The index is only as good as `aliases:`, and 389 of 497 pages have none.**
    Matching is exact-phrase on a page's title, and a title is almost never how
    a thing is written in a source. `wiki/interests/opie-and-anthony` is the
    worked case: the archive names it 61 times — "o&a" 8, "opie" 25, "cumia"
    10, "@OpieRadio" 18 — and the exact string "opie and anthony" appears
    **zero** times, so `coverage` reports the corpus silent on it. This is not
    a tuning problem, it is missing frontmatter, and populating `aliases:` is
    worth more to this campaign than any further work on the matcher.
    `entities --missing` is the list.

Why `check` never fails
-----------------------
The reciprocal debt is real work but it is not a corruption: a one-way edge
still carries its claim, and the target page is merely poorer for not having
it. A gate that went red on it would block every unrelated commit until 40
pages had been read, and CLAUDE.md is explicit about what happens to a
mandatory step that blocks unrelated work — it acquires an escape hatch and
stops being mandatory. `bin/wiki-connect check` already warns per-edge; this
prints the total so the trend is visible.
"""
import os, re, sys, json, collections

ROOT = os.path.dirname(os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__)))
WIKI = os.path.join(ROOT, "wiki")

# Corpora this tool knows how to read, keyed by the `sources:` path a page cites.
#
# `family` exists because the two message exports are NOT two forms of one
# corpus, which is what CROSSLINK_CAMPAIGN.md assumed before anybody measured
# it. Overlap on (timestamp, first 120 normalised chars), 2026-09-04:
#
#     dox-scan   215,057 unique rows        csv   174,775 unique rows
#     shared     124,379                    csv-only  50,396   dox-only  90,678
#
# So each holds a third of the record the other does not, and reading either
# alone loses it. They are deduped against each other when both are in play —
# `family` is what says they may be — and the dedupe is reported rather than
# silent, because a row count is the first thing a session reads off a scan.
CORPORA = {
    "raw/self/twitter/archive.jsonl": {
        "kind": "jsonl", "date": "created_at", "text": "text", "family": None,
        "note": "public broadcast; no counterparty, no inbound",
    },
    "raw/self/dox-scan/all_imessages_complete_dump.txt": {
        "kind": "doxdump", "family": "messages",
        "note": "direction reliable; 2015-2025 with 2012-2014 and 2022 empty, no 2026",
    },
    "raw/self/message-csv/MASTER_MESSAGES_DB_DUMP.csv": {
        "kind": "msgcsv", "family": "messages",
        "note": "the only message corpus covering 2026; 79% of Sent rows carry no handle",
    },
}

# ---- THE MORATORIUM IS ENFORCED HERE, NOT REMEMBERED -----------------------
#
# CLAUDE.md's standing directive stops the record on Annie advancing: no new
# narrative, event, synthesis or typed-edge claim about her. A tool whose whole
# job is to hand a session pages to write edges onto must not put hers in the
# queue, and must not answer a scan of one — a scan output *is* a worklist, and
# the pilot's queue surfaced her page at position five.
#
# Same shape as `bin/wiki-plain` in being a refusal rather than a warning, and
# deliberately NARROWER in what it refuses — see under_moratorium() for the
# measurement behind that. Only the operator changes it.
MORATORIUM = re.compile(r"\b(annie|ulmer)", re.I)


def under_moratorium(txt):
    """True for a page that IS about her — not for one that mentions her.

    **This threshold is deliberately narrower than `bin/wiki-plain`'s and the
    difference is not an oversight.** That tool forbids her name appearing in
    any file under `plain/` at all, so a page naming her three times cannot get
    a twin without either naming her or distorting the page; its INCIDENTAL=2
    body-mention rule follows from that and is correct there.

    Here the risk is different. The directive forbids writing new claims *about
    Annie*. Crosslinking `wiki/self/tattoos` to a Misfits tweet does not do
    that. Measured 2026-09-04: porting wiki-plain's rule across would refuse
    **197 of 497 pages** — including `interests/golf`, `self/tattoos` and
    `music/aliases/sloppp` — because she is a decade-long presence who gets
    mentioned in passing everywhere. That is 40% of the wiki withheld from the
    campaign for no safety gained, since none of those edges would be about her.

    So the refusal is scoped to her own pages, by title and aliases. The rest
    of the directive is a constraint on **what a session writes**, not on what
    it may look at, and `scan` prints the reminder on any page that names her.
    """
    fm, _ = split_fm(txt)
    head = (scalar(fm, "title") or "") + " " + " ".join(inline_list(fm, "aliases"))
    return bool(MORATORIUM.search(head))


# Pages that ARE about her, computed once at startup from the same test that
# refuses a scan of one. Held as a module global because render_hits needs it
# per candidate and load_pages() is not free.
MORATORIUM_TARGETS = set()


def note_moratorium_targets(pages):
    MORATORIUM_TARGETS.clear()
    for path, txt in pages.items():
        if under_moratorium(txt):
            MORATORIUM_TARGETS.add(path[:-3])
    return MORATORIUM_TARGETS


def mentions_moratorium(txt):
    _fm, body = split_fm(txt)
    return len(MORATORIUM.findall(body))


# Pages written by a generator, where a hand-added inverse edge is destroyed on
# the next run and fails that generator's own check. An edge INTO one of these
# is legitimate and is simply not reciprocable by hand: the tool reports them
# apart from the debt rather than counting work nobody may do. Keep in step
# with CLAUDE.md's list of generated surfaces.
GENERATED = {
    "wiki/meta/testimony-veracity",
    "wiki/meta/skills",
    "wiki/meta/readers-digest",
    "wiki/meta/digest",
    "wiki/meta/recent-activity",
    "wiki/meta/open-questions",
    "wiki/health/intake-ledger",
}

# Name tokens too common to carry a match on their own. A page whose entire
# name is one of these can only be found by reading.
STOPNAMES = {
    "dan", "frank", "mom", "dad", "mother", "father", "aunt", "uncle", "the",
    "and", "one", "two", "new", "old", "big", "red", "man", "boy", "girl",
    "annoying", "bub", "md", "al", "bill", "brian", "jess", "jimmy", "del",
    "felix", "clyde", "marla", "manuel", "bud", "chip", "buddy", "junior",
}

FM_LIST_RE = re.compile(r'^\s*-\s+(.+?)\s*$')


def split_fm(txt):
    if txt.startswith("---"):
        e = txt.find("\n---", 3)
        if e > 0:
            return txt[3:e], txt[e + 4:]
    return "", txt


def load_pages():
    pages = {}
    for root, _dirs, files in os.walk(WIKI):
        for f in sorted(files):
            if not f.endswith(".md"):
                continue
            p = os.path.relpath(os.path.join(root, f), ROOT)
            with open(os.path.join(root, f), encoding="utf-8", errors="replace") as fh:
                pages[p] = fh.read()
    return pages


def scalar(fm, key):
    m = re.search(r'^%s:\s*(.+?)\s*$' % re.escape(key), fm, re.M)
    if not m:
        return None
    v = m.group(1).strip()
    if v.startswith(("'", '"')) and v.endswith(("'", '"')) and len(v) > 1:
        v = v[1:-1]
    return v or None


def inline_list(fm, key):
    """Read `key: ["a", "b"]` or an indented `- ` block, at any nesting."""
    out = []
    m = re.search(r'^\s*%s:\s*\[(.*?)\]\s*$' % re.escape(key), fm, re.M | re.S)
    if m:
        # Split on commas and strip quotes per item. An earlier alternation-based
        # regex left the quotes on every entry after the first — the third branch
        # matched ` "Lexie"` whole — which silently demoted every quoted @handle
        # to a low-confidence single token, because the `startswith("@")` test
        # was looking at `"@alexisarmel"`. It cost 83 of 84 matching rows on one
        # page before anybody noticed, so this stays boring on purpose.
        for part in m.group(1).split(","):
            v = part.strip().strip('"\'').strip()
            if v:
                out.append(v)
        return out
    m = re.search(r'^(\s*)%s:\s*$' % re.escape(key), fm, re.M)
    if m:
        indent = len(m.group(1))
        for line in fm[m.end():].splitlines():
            if not line.strip():
                continue
            cur = len(line) - len(line.lstrip())
            if cur <= indent:
                break
            lm = FM_LIST_RE.match(line)
            if lm:
                out.append(lm.group(1).strip().strip('"\''))
    return out


def edges(fm):
    """[(page, type), ...] from a `connections:` block."""
    out, cur = [], None
    for line in fm.splitlines():
        m = re.match(r'^\s*-\s+page:\s*(.+?)\s*$', line)
        if m:
            cur = [m.group(1).strip().strip('"\''), None]
            out.append(cur)
            continue
        m = re.match(r'^\s+type:\s*(.+?)\s*$', line)
        if m and cur is not None:
            cur[1] = m.group(1).strip().strip('"\'')
    return [(p, t) for p, t in out]


INVERSE = {
    "causes": "caused-by", "caused-by": "causes",
    "evidences": "evidenced-by", "evidenced-by": "evidences",
    "instantiates": "instance-of", "instance-of": "instantiates",
    "precedes": "follows", "follows": "precedes",
    "supplies": "supplied-by", "supplied-by": "supplies",
    "component-of": "contains", "contains": "component-of",
}
SYMMETRIC = {"contradicts", "parallels", "mirrors", "co-occurs"}
NO_INVERSE = {"escalates", "resolves", "contextualizes"}


def title_of(path, fm):
    t = scalar(fm, "title")
    if t:
        return t
    ib = re.search(r'^infobox:\s*$', fm, re.M)
    if ib:
        n = re.search(r'^\s+name:\s*(.+?)\s*$', fm[ib.end():], re.M)
        if n:
            return n.group(1).strip().strip('"\'')
    return os.path.basename(path)[:-3].replace("-", " ")


def build_index(pages):
    """slug -> {names: [(string, confidence)], domain, title}"""
    idx = {}
    for path, txt in sorted(pages.items()):
        fm, _ = split_fm(txt)
        slug = path[:-3]
        title = title_of(path, fm)
        names = collections.OrderedDict()

        def add(s, conf):
            s = (s or "").strip()
            if not s or len(s) < 3:
                return
            if s.lower() in STOPNAMES:
                return
            if s.lower() not in names or conf == "high":
                names[s.lower()] = (s, conf)

        # The page's own name, and the slug read as words.
        for cand in (title, os.path.basename(slug).replace("-", " ")):
            toks = [t for t in re.split(r'\s+', cand) if t]
            conf = "high" if len(toks) > 1 else "low"
            add(cand, conf)
        for a in inline_list(fm, "aliases"):
            toks = [t for t in re.split(r'\s+', a) if t]
            add(a, "high" if (a.startswith("@") or len(toks) > 1) else "low")
        for h in inline_list(fm, "handles"):
            if h.startswith("@"):
                add(h, "high")
        idx[slug] = {
            "names": list(names.values()),
            "domain": scalar(fm, "domain") or "?",
            "title": title,
            "limit": self_limit_score(txt),
        }
    return idx


def _mine_messages():
    """The message-dump reader, imported rather than rewritten.

    `bin/mine-messages` already carries the three properties that make naive
    reading of that dump wrong — multi-line records reassembled, curly quotes
    folded, direction trustworthy only in this file — and every one of them had
    already produced a false measurement before it was written. A second reader
    here would inherit none of that and would look identical from outside,
    which is the failure mode CLAUDE.md's Phase 2 note warns about in advance:
    "a reader written without them produces confident wrong candidates".
    """
    import importlib.machinery, importlib.util
    p = os.path.join(ROOT, "bin", "mine-messages")
    loader = importlib.machinery.SourceFileLoader("_mine_messages", p)
    spec = importlib.util.spec_from_loader("_mine_messages", loader)
    mod = importlib.util.module_from_spec(spec)
    loader.exec_module(mod)
    return mod


_CORPUS_CACHE = {}


def load_corpus(rel):
    """[(timestamp, text, meta), ...] for one corpus. Memoised.

    Memoised because it stopped being cheap. The twitter archive is 1.3 MB and
    was re-read per page without anybody noticing; the message dump is 217,573
    records and re-reading it per page in a `--queue` run would have turned a
    scan into a coffee break.
    """
    if rel in _CORPUS_CACHE:
        return _CORPUS_CACHE[rel]
    spec = CORPORA[rel]
    kind = spec["kind"]
    path = os.path.join(ROOT, rel)
    rows = []
    if kind == "jsonl":
        datekey, textkey = spec["date"], spec["text"]
        with open(path, encoding="utf-8", errors="replace") as fh:
            for line in fh:
                line = line.strip()
                if not line:
                    continue
                try:
                    d = json.loads(line)
                except ValueError:
                    continue
                rows.append((d.get(datekey, ""), d.get(textkey, "") or "",
                             d.get("url", "")))
    elif kind == "doxdump":
        mm = _mine_messages()
        for r in mm.records(path):
            rows.append(("%s %s" % (r["date"], r["time"]),
                         mm.norm(r["text"]),
                         "%s %s" % (r["dir"], r["handle"] or "-")))
    elif kind == "msgcsv":
        # Direction is present and split nearly evenly here (88,988 Sent /
        # 86,370 Received, 2026-09-04) — but 69,869 of those Sent rows carry no
        # contact_handle at all, so this corpus can say WHAT Dan wrote and, four
        # times in five, not to whom. That is the trap to carry: it is sound for
        # a corpus-wide claim and unsound for a per-relationship one.
        mm = _mine_messages()
        import csv as _csv
        _csv.field_size_limit(10 ** 9)
        with open(path, encoding="utf-8", errors="replace", newline="") as fh:
            for r in _csv.DictReader(fh):
                txt = r.get("text") or ""
                if not txt.strip():
                    continue
                rows.append(((r.get("timestamp") or "").strip(),
                             mm.norm(txt),
                             "%s %s" % (r.get("direction") or "?",
                                        (r.get("contact_handle") or "-").strip() or "-")))
    _CORPUS_CACHE[rel] = rows
    return rows


def dedupe_key(ts, text):
    """Two exports of the same message must count once. See CORPORA above."""
    return (ts.strip(), re.sub(r"\s+", " ", text).strip().lower()[:120])


TOKEN_RE = re.compile(r"[a-z0-9@']+")

# Words too common to serve as the trigger token for a name. Only used to pick
# WHICH token of a multi-word name gets indexed — never to reject a match.
TRIGGER_STOP = set(
    "the a an and or of in on at to for is it my me you i we he she they them "
    "that this with his her from as by be was were are am not no yes do did"
    .split())


class Matcher:
    """Name lookup by inverted token index rather than one giant alternation.

    The alternation worked at 1.3 MB and did not survive the message corpus.
    Measured 2026-09-04 over the 217,573-record dump: a single `re.search` per
    row against the high band (a 19,418-character alternation) took **50.1s**.
    The same pass through a token index takes **0.6s** for the same 862 rows —
    80x, and the difference between a tool a session runs and one it works
    around.

    The index maps one trigger token per name to the names carrying it, so a
    row is only tested against names sharing a word with it. The trigger is the
    longest non-stopword token, as a cheap stand-in for the rarest. Every
    surviving candidate is then confirmed with a real word-boundary regex, so
    the index only decides what to *test* and never what matches.
    """

    def __init__(self, idx):
        self.owner = {}
        self.conf = {}
        self.trigger = collections.defaultdict(list)
        self._rx = {}
        for slug, meta in idx.items():
            for name, conf in meta["names"]:
                key = name.lower()
                self.owner.setdefault(key, set()).add(slug)
                if conf == "high" or key not in self.conf:
                    self.conf[key] = conf
        for key in self.conf:
            toks = [t for t in TOKEN_RE.findall(key) if t not in TRIGGER_STOP]
            if not toks:
                toks = TOKEN_RE.findall(key)
            if not toks:
                continue
            self.trigger[max(toks, key=len)].append(key)

    def _boundary(self, key):
        rx = self._rx.get(key)
        if rx is None:
            esc = re.escape(key)
            pat = (esc + r"\b") if key.startswith("@") else (r"\b" + esc + r"\b")
            rx = self._rx[key] = re.compile(pat)
        return rx

    def find(self, text):
        """[(name, conf), ...] — every indexed name this text contains."""
        low = text.lower()
        toks = set(TOKEN_RE.findall(low))
        out = []
        for t in toks & self.trigger.keys():
            for key in self.trigger[t]:
                if key in low and self._boundary(key).search(low):
                    out.append((key, self.conf[key]))
        return out

    def names_rx(self, names):
        """A boundary regex over an explicit name list — the own-name filter."""
        if not names:
            return None
        return re.compile("|".join(
            (re.escape(n.lower()) + r"\b") if n.startswith("@")
            else (r"\b" + re.escape(n.lower()) + r"\b") for n in names), re.I)


_INDEX_CACHE = {}


def corpus_index(rels, matcher):
    """One pass over the union of `rels`. Memoised per corpus set.

    Returns (rows, hits, slug_rows):
      rows      [(timestamp, text, meta, corpus_basename), ...] deduped
      hits      {row_index: [(name, conf, slug), ...]}
      slug_rows {slug: [row_index, ...]}

    Built as an index rather than re-scanned per page because both questions
    the campaign asks — "which corpora name this page" (coverage) and "inside
    the rows that name it, what else is named" (scan --against) — are lookups
    against the same pass. Before this, `coverage` over 497 pages ran the whole
    corpus once per page.
    """
    key = tuple(sorted(rels))
    if key in _INDEX_CACHE:
        return _INDEX_CACHE[key]
    families = {}
    for rel in key:
        families.setdefault(CORPORA[rel].get("family"), []).append(rel)
    rows, dropped = [], 0
    for fam, rels_in in sorted(families.items(), key=lambda kv: (kv[0] or "")):
        seen = set() if (fam and len(rels_in) > 1) else None
        for rel in rels_in:
            base = os.path.basename(rel)
            for ts, text, meta in load_corpus(rel):
                if seen is not None:
                    k = dedupe_key(ts, text)
                    if k in seen:
                        dropped += 1
                        continue
                    seen.add(k)
                rows.append((ts, text, meta, base))
    hits, slug_rows = {}, collections.defaultdict(list)
    for i, (_ts, text, _m, _b) in enumerate(rows):
        found = matcher.find(text)
        if not found:
            continue
        per = []
        for name, conf in found:
            for slug in matcher.owner.get(name, ()):
                per.append((name, conf, slug))
                slug_rows[slug].append(i)
        hits[i] = per
    out = (rows, hits, dict(slug_rows), dropped)
    _INDEX_CACHE[key] = out
    return out


def page_range(fm):
    return scalar(fm, "date_range_start"), scalar(fm, "date_range_end")


def cmd_entities(argv):
    pages = load_pages()
    idx = build_index(pages)
    if argv and argv[0] == "--missing":
        return cmd_entities_missing(idx, pages)
    filt = argv[0].lower() if argv else None
    n = 0
    for slug in sorted(idx):
        meta = idx[slug]
        if filt and filt not in slug.lower() and filt not in meta["title"].lower():
            continue
        names = ", ".join("%s[%s]" % (s, c[0]) for s, c in meta["names"])
        print("%-58s %s" % (slug, names))
        n += 1
    print("\n%d page(s) indexed" % n)
    return 0


SELF_LIMIT = re.compile(
    r"\b(complete record|the complete|only (?:source|evidence|record|trace)|"
    r"everything known|rests (?:entirely )?on|remains description|"
    r"no (?:ledger entries|corroboration|primary source|dated evidence|message corpus)|"
    r"blind window|testimony alone|his own telling|nothing else in the corpus|"
    r"not (?:datable|checkable|dated)|has never been checked|unmeasured)\b", re.I)


def self_limit_score(txt):
    """How loudly a page states a checkable limit about its own evidence.

    Measured 2026-09-04: 157 of 497 pages say something of this kind, and every
    finding of the pilot pass landed on one — four of them falsifying the claim
    outright. It is the difference between a queue and a pile, so it is computed
    rather than left to a session to notice.
    """
    return len(SELF_LIMIT.findall(txt))


def scan_page(slug, pages, idx, matcher, limit=3, against=None):
    """Candidates for one page. Two modes, and they ask different questions.

    **Cited mode** (no `against`) reads the corpora the page's own `sources:`
    name. The page was assembled *from* this corpus, so every row in its date
    range is arguably its business, and the question is "what did the writer
    not carry across?"

    **Against mode** reads a corpus the page does NOT cite, and the date range
    alone is not enough to scope it: a person's page spanning 2009-2025 would
    match every entity in seventeen years of archive, which is the same answer
    for every such page and therefore no answer at all. So it first finds the
    rows that name **this page** — its own title, aliases and handles, at high
    confidence — and looks only inside those. The question becomes "in what
    this corpus says about X, what else does it name?", which is the question
    the source-mention obligation is actually about.

    A page whose names never appear is a fast, real negative: the corpus is
    silent on it, and that is worth knowing before anybody reads further.
    """
    path = slug + ".md"
    txt = pages[path]
    fm, _ = split_fm(txt)
    srcs = inline_list(fm, "sources")
    if against:
        corpora = list(against)
    else:
        corpora = [s for s in srcs if s in CORPORA]
    if not corpora:
        return None
    start, end = page_range(fm)
    # A missing date range used to refuse an --against scan. That was written
    # before the name filter below existed, when the range was the only thing
    # scoping the read; with the filter doing the scoping it blocked 246 pages
    # for nothing, and the rows it excluded — a page's subject named outside
    # the era the page covers — are among the more interesting results.
    # Unbounded now, and the output says so.

    linked = {p for p, _ in edges(fm)}
    linked.add(slug)
    for m in re.finditer(r'(wiki/[a-z0-9\-/]+)', txt):
        linked.add(m.group(1))

    rows, allhits, slug_rows, _dropped = corpus_index(corpora, matcher)

    if against:
        if not [n for n, c in idx[slug]["names"] if c == "high"]:
            return "NONAMES"
        # Only rows that name THIS page, and only on a high-confidence name of
        # it — a bare first name would drag in every row about anybody.
        candidate_rows = [i for i in slug_rows.get(slug, ())
                          if any(s == slug and c == "high"
                                 for _n, c, s in allhits.get(i, ()))]
    else:
        candidate_rows = sorted(allhits)

    hits = collections.defaultdict(list)
    matched_rows = 0
    for i in candidate_rows:
        ts, text, meta, base = rows[i]
        if start and ts[:10] < start:
            continue
        if end and ts[:10] > end:
            continue
        matched_rows += 1
        seen = set()
        for name, conf, tgt in allhits.get(i, ()):
            if tgt in linked or (name, tgt) in seen:
                continue
            seen.add((name, tgt))
            hits[tgt].append((ts[:10], name, conf, text, meta, base,
                              len(matcher.owner.get(name, ()))))
    if against:
        hits["__rows__"] = matched_rows
    return hits


def render_hits(slug, hits, idx, limit=3, show_low=False):
    """Candidates, ranked by how much the TARGET page needs them.

    Two orderings were tried. By mention count the head of the list is whatever
    string happens to recur — 48 SLOPPP shares, 18 hits on the word "music" —
    and the page whose own stated limit the source falsifies sits below the
    fold. By self-limit score first, the pages that told you what they do not
    know come first, which is where every finding of the pilot pass landed.
    """
    if not hits:
        print("  (nothing unlinked found)")
        return 0
    rows_matched = hits.pop("__rows__", None)
    if rows_matched is not None:
        print("  %d row(s) in this corpus name this page" % rows_matched)
        if rows_matched == 0:
            print("  (the corpus is silent on it — a real negative, worth recording)")
            return 0
    shown = {}
    suppressed = 0
    refused = 0
    for tgt, rows in hits.items():
        # THE MORATORIUM APPLIES TO A CANDIDATE TARGET, NOT ONLY TO THE PAGE
        # BEING SCANNED. It was enforced in two places — scanning her page, and
        # the --queue — and in neither of the two places where her page comes
        # back as somewhere to WRITE. A candidate list is a worklist whichever
        # column her name lands in. Found 2026-09-04 scanning the concert
        # record, which offered her page with 101 mentions.
        if MORATORIUM_TARGETS and tgt in MORATORIUM_TARGETS:
            refused += 1
            continue
        keep = rows if show_low else [r for r in rows if r[2] == "high"]
        if keep:
            shown[tgt] = keep
        else:
            suppressed += 1
    if not shown:
        print("  (nothing unlinked found%s)" % ("" if show_low else " above low confidence"))
        if suppressed:
            print("  %d low-confidence target(s) suppressed — --low to see them" % suppressed)
        return 0
    ranked = sorted(
        shown.items(),
        key=lambda kv: (-idx[kv[0]].get("limit", 0), -len(kv[1]), kv[0]),
    )
    for tgt, rows in ranked:
        conf = "high" if any(r[2] == "high" for r in rows) else "low"
        lim = idx[tgt].get("limit", 0)
        flag = ("  ** states %d limit(s) about its own evidence **" % lim) if lim else ""
        # A string two pages both claim is not evidence for either of them. The
        # index knows this and used to say nothing: @alexisarmel is in
        # alexis-armel's aliases AND in annie-ulmer's infobox handles, so 85
        # rows about one person were offered as 101 mentions of the other, with
        # no mark on the output to read them by.
        contested = sorted({r[1] for r in rows if r[6] > 1})
        if contested:
            flag += ("  !! %d name(s) contested by another page: %s"
                     % (len(contested), ", ".join(contested[:3])))
        print("  [%s] %-46s %d mention(s)  <%s>%s"
              % (conf, tgt, len(rows), idx[tgt]["domain"], flag))
        for date, tok, _c, text, meta, base, nown in rows[:limit]:
            snip = re.sub(r'\s+', ' ', text).strip()
            if len(snip) > 132:
                snip = snip[:129] + "..."
            # A truncated tweet permalink is 22 characters of "https://x.com/dan"
            # and tells a reader nothing; a message's direction and handle is
            # the whole point of reading it.
            m = "" if (meta or "").startswith("http") else (meta or "")[:22]
            print("        %s  %-16s %-22s %s" % (date, tok[:16], m, snip))
        if len(rows) > limit:
            print("        ... %d more" % (len(rows) - limit))
    if suppressed:
        print("  (%d low-confidence target(s) suppressed — --low to see them)" % suppressed)
    if refused:
        print("  (%d target(s) withheld under the standing directive in CLAUDE.md)"
              % refused)
    return len(ranked)


def cmd_entities_missing(idx, pages):
    """Pages carrying no alias, so the index can only find them by exact title.

    Ranked by how likely that is to cost something: a page whose title is a
    single token is invisible anyway (it scores `low` and is suppressed), and a
    page whose title is a multi-word proper noun is exactly the kind a source
    abbreviates. 395 of 497 pages are in here as of 2026-09-04.
    """
    rows = []
    for slug in sorted(idx):
        fm, _ = split_fm(pages[slug + ".md"])
        if inline_list(fm, "aliases"):
            continue
        title = idx[slug]["title"]
        toks = len(title.split())
        # A multi-word title is abbreviated in sources; a one-word title is
        # already unfindable for a different reason. Both need aliases, and the
        # first kind is where a scan is silently returning a false negative.
        rows.append((0 if toks > 1 else 1, -idx[slug].get("limit", 0), slug, title, toks))
    rows.sort()
    for kind, neglim, slug, title, toks in rows:
        tag = "multi-word title, likely abbreviated in sources" if kind == 0 else "single token"
        lim = -neglim
        print("  %-52s %-34s %s%s" % (slug[:52], title[:34], tag,
                                      ("  ** %d self-limit claim(s) **" % lim) if lim else ""))
    print("\n%d page(s) carry no aliases: — the index can only match their exact title"
          % len(rows))
    return 0


def cmd_scan(argv):
    pages = load_pages()
    note_moratorium_targets(pages)
    idx = build_index(pages)
    matcher = Matcher(idx)

    show_low = "--low" in argv
    argv = [a for a in argv if a != "--low"]
    against = None
    if "--against" in argv:
        i = argv.index("--against")
        if i + 1 >= len(argv):
            print("scan: --against needs a corpus path", file=sys.stderr)
            return 1
        corpus = argv[i + 1]
        if corpus not in CORPORA:
            print("scan: no reader for %s\n  readable: %s"
                  % (corpus, ", ".join(sorted(CORPORA))), file=sys.stderr)
            return 1
        against = [corpus]
        argv = argv[:i] + argv[i + 2:]

    targets = []
    if argv and argv[0] == "--all":
        targets = [s for s in sorted(idx) if s.startswith("wiki/self/twitter")]
    elif argv and argv[0] == "--queue":
        # The campaign queue: pages that state a limit about their own evidence,
        # loudest first. Requires --against, because the point of the queue is
        # to check a page against a corpus it does NOT already cite.
        if not against:
            print("scan --queue: needs --against <corpus> — the queue exists to "
                  "check a page against a corpus it does not already cite",
                  file=sys.stderr)
            return 1
        n = int(argv[1]) if len(argv) > 1 and argv[1].isdigit() else 25
        scored = []
        for slug, meta in idx.items():
            if not meta.get("limit"):
                continue
            if under_moratorium(pages[slug + ".md"]):
                continue
            fm, _ = split_fm(pages[slug + ".md"])
            if against[0] in inline_list(fm, "sources"):
                continue          # already cites it; ordinary scan covers that
            scored.append((-meta["limit"], slug))
        targets = [s for _, s in sorted(scored)][:n]
        if not targets:
            print("scan --queue: nothing eligible for %s" % against[0])
            return 0
    elif argv:
        for a in argv:
            s = a[:-3] if a.endswith(".md") else a
            if s + ".md" not in pages:
                print("scan: no such page: %s" % a, file=sys.stderr)
                return 1
            targets.append(s)
    else:
        print(USAGE)
        return 1

    total, skipped, refused = 0, 0, 0
    for slug in targets:
        if under_moratorium(pages[slug + ".md"]):
            print("== %s — REFUSED: standing directive in CLAUDE.md. This tool "
                  "hands out pages to write edges onto; that record does not "
                  "advance." % slug)
            refused += 1
            continue
        _m = mentions_moratorium(pages[slug + ".md"])
        if _m:
            print("   ! this page names her %d time(s). Scanning it is fine; the "
                  "directive governs what you WRITE — no new claim about her, "
                  "on this page or any other." % _m)
        hits = scan_page(slug, pages, idx, matcher, against=against)
        if hits is None:
            print("== %s — cites no corpus this tool can read" % slug)
            continue
        if hits == "NONAMES":
            print("== %s — SKIPPED: no unambiguous name to search for (a bare "
                  "first name would match anybody)" % slug)
            skipped += 1
            continue
        _fm2, _ = split_fm(pages[slug + ".md"])
        _s2, _e2 = page_range(_fm2)
        _scope = ""
        if against:
            _scope = ("  [against %s%s]"
                      % (os.path.basename(against[0]),
                         "" if (_s2 and _e2) else ", UNBOUNDED — page has no date range"))
        print("== %s%s" % (slug, _scope))
        total += render_hits(slug, hits, idx, show_low=show_low)
    bits = []
    if skipped:
        bits.append("%d unscannable (no date range, or no unambiguous name)" % skipped)
    if refused:
        bits.append("%d refused under the standing directive" % refused)
    tail = (", " + ", ".join(bits)) if bits else ""
    print("\n%d candidate edge(s) across %d page(s)%s — verify each by reading "
          "the rows above" % (total, len(targets) - skipped - refused, tail))
    return 0


def cmd_coverage(argv):
    """Which readable corpora actually name a page, and how loudly.

    Built after the first queue run, where ten of twelve pages came back with
    the corpus silent on them. That is a correct and cheap answer, but paying
    a full scan to get it is the wrong order of operations: matching a page to
    the corpus that has something to say about it is the triage step, and this
    is it. Rows, not candidates.

    Each corpus is indexed once and every page reads its own rows out of that
    index. The first version ran the whole corpus once per page, which was
    invisible at 1.3 MB and would have been 497 passes over 217,573 records the
    moment the message dump became readable.

    `--silent` inverts it: the pages a corpus does NOT name. On a corpus a page
    cites, that is the interesting half — the page claims a source that never
    mentions it, which is either a missing alias or a citation doing no work.
    """
    pages = load_pages()
    note_moratorium_targets(pages)
    idx = build_index(pages)
    matcher = Matcher(idx)
    only = [a for a in argv if not a.startswith("-")]
    silent = "--silent" in argv
    prefix = only[0] if only else "wiki/"
    per_corpus = {}
    for rel in sorted(CORPORA):
        rows, hits, slug_rows, _d = corpus_index([rel], matcher)
        per_corpus[rel] = (rows, hits, slug_rows)
    out, quiet = [], []
    for slug in sorted(idx):
        if not slug.startswith(prefix.rstrip("/")):
            continue
        txt = pages[slug + ".md"]
        if under_moratorium(txt):
            continue
        fm, _ = split_fm(txt)
        if not [n for n, c in idx[slug]["names"] if c == "high"]:
            continue
        start, end = page_range(fm)
        cited = inline_list(fm, "sources")
        per = {}
        for rel, (rows, hits, slug_rows) in per_corpus.items():
            n = 0
            for i in slug_rows.get(slug, ()):
                if not any(s == slug and c == "high" for _nm, c, s in hits.get(i, ())):
                    continue
                ts = rows[i][0]
                if start and ts[:10] < start:
                    continue
                if end and ts[:10] > end:
                    continue
                n += 1
            per[rel] = (n, rel in cited)
        best = max((v[0] for v in per.values()), default=0)
        if best:
            out.append((best, slug, per, idx[slug].get("limit", 0)))
        else:
            quiet.append((slug, [r for r in cited if r in CORPORA],
                          idx[slug].get("limit", 0),
                          scalar(fm, "page_type") or "?"))
    if silent:
        # A synthesis or concept page is named by no corpus BY CONSTRUCTION —
        # "block unblock loop" is a finding this wiki coined, not a phrase
        # anybody texted. Reporting those beside a person the corpus really is
        # silent about would make the number mean two different things at once,
        # so the type is printed and the tail counts them apart.
        COINED = {"synthesis", "concept"}
        quiet.sort(key=lambda r: (-r[2], r[0]))
        n_cited = n_coined = 0
        for slug, cites, lim, ptype in quiet:
            tag = ("  cites %s and is not named in it"
                   % ",".join(os.path.basename(c) for c in cites)) if cites else ""
            if ptype in COINED:
                n_coined += 1
            elif cites:
                n_cited += 1
            print("%-52s %6d  %-10s%s" % (slug[:52], lim, ptype[:10], tag))
        print("\n%d page(s) no readable corpus names. %d are synthesis or concept "
              "pages, whose names the corpus cannot contain; %d of the rest cite a "
              "corpus anyway — a missing alias, or a citation doing no work"
              % (len(quiet), n_coined, n_cited))
        return 0
    out.sort(key=lambda r: (-r[3], -r[0]))
    print("%-52s %6s %6s  %s" % ("page", "rows", "limits", "corpus (* = already cited)"))
    for best, slug, per, lim in out:
        for rel, (n, cites) in sorted(per.items(), key=lambda kv: -kv[1][0]):
            if n:
                print("%-52s %6d %6d  %s%s"
                      % (slug[:52], n, lim, os.path.basename(rel), "*" if cites else ""))
    print("\n%d page(s) named by at least one readable corpus, "
          "self-limit claims first; %d named by none (--silent)"
          % (len(out), len(quiet)))
    print("An unstarred row is a page a corpus talks about and that does not "
          "cite it — the crosslink queue proper.")
    return 0


def cmd_reciprocal(argv):
    pages = load_pages()
    filt = argv[0] if argv else None
    fms = {p: split_fm(t)[0] for p, t in pages.items()}
    debt = []
    generated = []
    for path, fm in sorted(fms.items()):
        slug = path[:-3]
        if filt and not slug.startswith(filt.rstrip("/")):
            continue
        for tgt, typ in edges(fm):
            if typ in NO_INVERSE:
                continue
            tf = fms.get(tgt + ".md")
            if tf is None:
                continue  # wiki-connect check owns unresolvable targets
            if tgt in GENERATED:
                generated.append((slug, typ, tgt))
                continue
            back = [t for p, t in edges(tf) if p == slug]
            if not back:
                debt.append((slug, typ, tgt, "no edge back"))
            else:
                want = INVERSE.get(typ, typ if typ in SYMMETRIC else None)
                if want and want not in back:
                    debt.append((slug, typ, tgt, "back-edge is '%s', expected '%s'" % (back[0], want)))
    for s, typ, t, why in debt:
        print("  %-44s --%s--> %-44s  %s" % (s, typ, t, why))
    print("\n%d edge(s) owe an inverse" % len(debt))
    if generated:
        print("\n%d edge(s) point at a GENERATED page and are not reciprocable by hand:" % len(generated))
        for s, typ, t in generated:
            print("  %-44s --%s--> %s" % (s, typ, t))
        print("  (a hand-added inverse there is destroyed on the next generator run)")
    return 0


def cmd_orphaned(argv):
    pages = load_pages()
    out = []
    for path, txt in sorted(pages.items()):
        fm, _ = split_fm(txt)
        srcs = [s for s in inline_list(fm, "sources") if s in CORPORA]
        if not srcs:
            continue
        n = len(edges(fm))
        out.append((n, path[:-3], srcs))
    for n, slug, srcs in sorted(out):
        flag = "  <-- no edges" if n == 0 else ""
        print("  %3d edges  %-52s %s%s" % (n, slug, ",".join(os.path.basename(s) for s in srcs), flag))
    print("\n%d page(s) cite a readable corpus" % len(out))
    return 0


def cmd_check(argv):
    pages = load_pages()
    fms = {p: split_fm(t)[0] for p, t in pages.items()}
    debt = 0
    for path, fm in fms.items():
        slug = path[:-3]
        for tgt, typ in edges(fm):
            if typ in NO_INVERSE:
                continue
            tf = fms.get(tgt + ".md")
            if tf is None or tgt in GENERATED:
                continue
            if not any(p == slug for p, _ in edges(tf)):
                debt += 1
    if debt:
        print("crosslink: %d one-way edge(s) owe an inverse — bin/wiki-crosslink reciprocal" % debt)
    else:
        print("crosslink: every typed edge is reciprocated")
    return 0


USAGE = """usage: wiki-crosslink <entities|coverage|scan|reciprocal|orphaned|check> [args]

  entities [filter]          the strings a scan can look for
  entities --missing         pages with no aliases: — the index's blind spot
  scan <page>|--all          unlinked entities in a page's own cited corpora
  scan --against <corpus>    ...against a corpus the page does NOT cite,
                             filtered to rows that name the page itself
  scan --queue [n] --against the campaign queue: pages that state a limit
                             about their own evidence, loudest first
       [--low]               include single-token name matches (mostly noise)
  coverage [prefix]          which readable corpora name a page, and how often
                             — the triage step, run this before scanning
       [--silent]            invert it: pages no corpus names, and which of
                             them cite one anyway
  reciprocal [prefix]        typed edges whose target does not link back
  orphaned                   corpus-backed pages and their edge counts
  check                      the reciprocal-debt banner (always exits 0)
"""


def main(argv):
    if not argv:
        print(USAGE)
        return 1
    cmd, rest = argv[0], argv[1:]
    fns = {"entities": cmd_entities, "scan": cmd_scan, "reciprocal": cmd_reciprocal,
           "orphaned": cmd_orphaned, "check": cmd_check, "coverage": cmd_coverage}
    if cmd not in fns:
        print(USAGE)
        return 1
    return fns[cmd](rest)


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main(sys.argv[1:]))
